<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gaspar de Carvajal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1500–<a href="/years/1584">1584</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gaspar de Carvajal was a Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-order">Dominican friar</a> who accompanied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-orellana">Francisco de Orellana</a>'s voyage down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a> in 1541–1542. His chronicle of the expedition describes large, densely settled chiefdoms along the river, such as that of Machiparo, and the women warriors who gave the Amazon its name. Long doubted, his picture of populous riverbank societies has been supported by modern archaeology.</p></div>
